WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.750
INTRO:
Over the past few decades, tech has transitioned

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:05.920
from being a luxury consumer product to a
necessity.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.180
From easy access to information and savings
in time, energy and effort to mobility, safety,

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:13.040
and of course entertainment.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.509
There's no doubt that tech has become an integral
part of all of our lives today, and that it’s

00:00:16.509 --> 00:00:17.509
pros far outweigh its cons.

00:00:17.509 --> 00:00:20.810
But, when we’re so dependent on a set of
products, it becomes very important to protect

00:00:20.810 --> 00:00:21.810
the source.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:26.619
Luckily, there’s thousands of tech companies
today and dozens of competitors in each sector,

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:31.519
so even if some companies went under, we would
have plenty of options to choose from right?

00:00:31.519 --> 00:00:35.930
Well, even though there’s thousands of tech
companies, there’s only a handful of companies

00:00:35.930 --> 00:00:40.710
that manufacture all the tech with the most
prominent one being Foxconn.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:47.030
Foxconn is a Taiwanese contract manufacturer
who assembles 40% of all global consumer electronics.

00:00:47.030 --> 00:00:51.300
Basically every tech company you can think
of works with Foxconn including Apple, Amazon,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.530
Dell, Google, Intel, Microsoft, and everyone
else.

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:59.489
But, despite their dominance, the ethics and
morality of the company are much more questionable.

00:00:59.489 --> 00:01:05.929
So, here’s how Foxconn became the modern
day plantation that we can’t live without.

00:01:05.929 --> 00:01:11.630
TERRY GOU:
Taking a look back, the story of Foxconn circles

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:16.350
back to a man named Terry Gou who was born
in Taipei County on October 18, 1950.

00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:21.039
His parents were originally from mainland
China, but they had fled to Taiwan near the

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:22.710
end of the Chinese Civil War.

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.859
In Taiwan, Terry’s father continued his
career as a police officer while Terry worked

00:01:26.859 --> 00:01:28.030
his way through school.

00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.539
With entrepreneurs, we often see polar ends
of the spectrum when it comes to education.

00:01:32.539 --> 00:01:36.799
Either they dropped out of school to start
a business or they got a PhD from MIT.

00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:38.920
But, Terry was a middle of the road student.

00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:42.929
He would end up attending Taipei University
of Marine Technology where he got his bachelors.

00:01:42.929 --> 00:01:46.649
It’s not clear what Terry majored in, but
it doesn’t seem to be too influential to

00:01:46.649 --> 00:01:51.040
his career as Terry ended up getting a low
level job at a rubber factory following college.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.219
For most of his early 20s, Terry found himself
working many such jobs, but despite his low

00:01:56.219 --> 00:02:00.490
wages, Terry was slowly able to build up some
capital thanks to his frugality.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:16.220
By the time he was 24, Terry had saved up
$7500 which he would use to start Hon Hai.

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:20.610
Terry manufactured plastic TV parts which
had quite a bit of demand, but it wasn’t

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:22.260
glamorous by any means.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.780
Hon Hai was originally run out of a rented
shed in Tucheng, and the staff consisted of

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.640
10 elderly workers who probably weren’t
able to find jobs anywhere else due to their

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.640
age.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.890
Despite the rough start, the TV parts were
enough to make ends meet until Terry received

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.080
his first breakthrough order.

00:02:37.080 --> 00:02:42.000
In 1980, Atari would ask Hon Hai to manufacture
their console joystick, and this would motivate

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.810
Terry to travel to the US and try to acquire
more western buyers.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:50.380
Terry commonly arrived at company headquarters
uninvited, and as you would guess, he had

00:02:50.380 --> 00:02:52.340
security called on him many times.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.100
Terry wasn’t phased by these experiences
though, and he would continue to hustle his

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:59.430
way into HQs and secure a couple of contracts
by the time he returned to Taiwan.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:03.900
These new western contracts substantially
helped Hon Hai’s financials, but this new

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.960
link with American companies was just the
beginning.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.510
FOXCONN:
In 1988, Terry would purchase the company’s

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.740
first formal factory in Shenzhen China.

00:03:14.740 --> 00:03:18.750
Since then, Terry has purchased and built
dozens of factories around the world, but

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:22.100
this original factory is still one of his
largest.

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:27.280
Anyway, in 1996, Terry would get a contract
from Compaq to manufacture desktop chassis.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.380
This quickly grew into contracts with HP and
IBM as well, but the nail in the coffin didn’t

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:35.680
come till 2000 when Foxconn inked a deal with
Apple.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:40.020
Foxconn had already manufactured parts for
Apple in the past, but this time, they were

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.570
contracted to produce the iMac itself.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:45.920
This project not only changed Foxconn’s
balance sheet, but it permanently changed

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:48.040
the quality of their manufacturing.

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:52.560
As we all know, Apple has high quality standards,
and meeting these standards was a prerequisite

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:54.850
to continue working with Apple.

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:58.319
Meeting Apple’s quality standards wasn’t
easy though because it wasn’t just a case

00:03:58.319 --> 00:04:02.070
of sourcing high quality materials and ensuring
excellent consistency.

00:04:02.070 --> 00:04:06.180
In many cases, Foxconn had to invent new manufacturing
procedures to make Apple’s slick design

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.940
aesthetic a reality.

00:04:07.940 --> 00:04:11.490
While this was often quite challenging, this
helped Foxconn make a name for themselves

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.850
in the manufacturing space.

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:17.060
They were no longer just another Asian manufacturer
that competed based on price.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:21.750
They were turning into the go to manufacturer
if you wanted customizability and quality.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:25.040
And this new role would really be put to the
test with the launch of the iPhone.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:30.940
Between 2009 and 2012, Apple went from accounting
for 25% of Foxconn’s revenue to a peak of

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:31.940
55%.

00:04:31.940 --> 00:04:35.660
Also, don’t forget, Apple was actually regularly
changing the iPhone’s design during this

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.990
time period, so keeping up with exploding
demand and constantly evolving design was

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:41.210
extremely challenging.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:45.181
But, by the time Foxconn got to the other
side of the mobile adoption curve, they were

00:04:45.181 --> 00:04:48.130
one of the most desirable manufacturers in
the world.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:52.340
Foxconn was receiving orders from every tech
company you can think of, and Foxconn’s

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:58.090
revenue exploded from $40 billion in 2009
to $140 billion by 2016.

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:02.150
In the meantime, Terry was using all these
new found profits to acquire company’s left

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.150
and right.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.410
But behind the scenes, the story of Foxconn
was completely different.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:12.330
THE TECH PLANTATION:
One of the biggest reasons that most people

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:17.270
have never heard about Foxconn is not because
they’re underrated, but because they actively

00:05:17.270 --> 00:05:18.610
try to fly under the radar.

00:05:18.610 --> 00:05:23.680
You see, the working conditions at Foxconn
aren’t particularly pleasant, and the less

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:26.280
media attention they can get, the better.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:29.180
First of all, most factory workers live at
the factories.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:33.130
On the brightside, this means that employees
don’t have to spend any money on housing,

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:38.190
food, recreation, and other basic amenities
as all of these are taken care of by Foxconn.

00:05:38.190 --> 00:05:42.010
Foxconn has gotten a bit more stingy over
the years forcing employees to pay for things

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:46.830
such as laundry, but for the most, if you’re
a Foxconn employee, your cost of living is

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.450
extremely low.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.270
The problem though is that unless you’re
sleeping, eating, or performing other basic

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:54.030
day to day functions, you’re working.

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.980
This means 12 hour shifts 6 days a week if
not 7 days a week.

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.919
Employees aren’t allowed to talk to each
other, and they get a 10 minute bathroom break

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:03.220
every 2 hours.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:06.570
Apple actually received a lot of backlash
for these working conditions in the early

00:06:06.570 --> 00:06:10.790
2010s, and they worked with Foxconn to reduce
these hours to 60 hours per week.

00:06:10.790 --> 00:06:13.310
But really, this doesn’t mean much.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:17.990
Sure, Foxconn employees are only required
to work 60 hours a week, but given that most

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:21.760
of them are in terrible financial situations,
they’re desperate to work all the overtime

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:24.350
they can which leads to 12 hour shifts once
again.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:28.570
At least, they’re getting paid for the overtime,
but again, this doesn’t really mean much

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:29.670
either.

00:06:29.670 --> 00:06:34.770
As of November 2021, the average Foxconn employee
was receiving a base monthly salary of a whopping

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:38.020
$314 before deductions.

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:41.199
Even if we assume that this only includes
40 hours of work per week, we’re looking

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:44.169
at just $1.96 per hour.

00:06:44.169 --> 00:06:49.010
If we assume a more realistic 60 hours per
week, we’re looking at $1.30 per hour.

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:54.050
After mandatory deductions, taxes, and pension
payments, this drops to a net take home income

00:06:54.050 --> 00:06:56.949
of a measly 98 cents per hour.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:07:00.770
But terrible working conditions and pay are
just scratching the surface.

00:07:00.770 --> 00:07:06.520
THE ETHICAL BORDER:
Despite basically operating a tech plantation,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.810
Foxconn is not breaking any laws.

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:13.260
They follow all the minimum wage and worker
requirements laid out by China and Taiwan.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:17.350
But, this doesn’t mean that they aren’t
crossing ethical boundaries.

00:07:17.350 --> 00:07:21.790
Aside from it being immoral to pay employees
$2 per hour and letting them work till exhaustion,

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:25.650
the most unethical result of Foxconn’s work
culture is literally death.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:30.050
No asian Foxconn factory worker chooses to
work there because they want to.

00:07:30.050 --> 00:07:34.310
Usually, people apply to Foxconn because they
have no choice, and this is their last means

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:35.760
of survival.

00:07:35.760 --> 00:07:41.300
But unfortunately, many would rather just
not live at all than continue working at Foxconn.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:45.759
Starting in 2010, dozens of factory workers
started jumping off the factory buildings,

00:07:45.759 --> 00:07:49.640
and this issue actually got so common that
Foxconn had to install nets on the sides of

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:51.180
their buildings to reduce deaths.

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:55.050
I don’t think I have to say anything else
about Foxconn’s work culture.

00:07:55.050 --> 00:07:58.169
Foxconn’s work environment isn’t the only
toxic part of their business either.

00:07:58.169 --> 00:08:03.139
Their business itself is literally toxic both
to workers and the environment.

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:07.040
It’s no secret that loads of toxic materials
and chemicals are used in the production of

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:08.040
electronics.

00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:13.960
Between 2010 and 2014, 13 workers under the
age of 20 developed leukemia.

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:17.570
Many have speculated that this is due to the
employees coming into contact with Benzene

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:22.320
in the factories, but Foxconn has repeatedly
denied this asserting that there is no contact

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:23.790
with Benzene.

00:08:23.790 --> 00:08:27.860
Even if this is true though, given the non
negligible number of leukemia cases, it seems

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:30.449
like Foxconn does play some role in this.

00:08:30.449 --> 00:08:34.620
Moving onto their environmental impact, their
most obvious form of pollution is of course

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:35.890
air pollution.

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:39.930
Running their factories requires a lot of
energy which in turn leads to a lot of air

00:08:39.930 --> 00:08:40.930
pollution.

00:08:40.930 --> 00:08:44.520
Foxconn has been working on installing solar
panels and transitioning to renewable energy,

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.710
but this is still in the early stages.

00:08:46.710 --> 00:08:50.480
Switching to renewable energy wouldn’t clean
Foxconn though as a large portion of their

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:51.860
pollution is water pollution.

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:56.210
Many of their factories draw water from nearby
lakes and rivers to use in the manufacturing

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:57.210
process.

00:08:57.210 --> 00:09:00.680
And then, they dump their polluted chemical
water back into the same lakes and rivers

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:04.060
ruining local environments and potentially
poisoning nearby residents.

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:09.000
So while Foxconn may not be breaking any legal
boundaries, their stance on ethical boundaries

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.230
is a lot more questionable.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:15.770
SUPPLY SHORTAGE:
Activists have tried to fight back against

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:19.910
Foxconn’s ethical issues for years now,
but it’s been difficult to make any real

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:20.910
progress.

00:09:20.910 --> 00:09:23.640
The supply shortage, however, is causing change.

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:27.960
The shortage has revealed the brutal reality
of relying so heavily on one company for the

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.330
world’s consumer tech.

00:09:29.330 --> 00:09:33.860
Foxconn’s clients are pushing Foxconn to
diversify outside of Asia, and they’re contracting

00:09:33.860 --> 00:09:36.380
out more and more work to other manufacturers.

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:40.630
While this doesn’t directly address the
ethical concerns regarding Foxconn, it does

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:42.850
indirectly solve many of these issues.

00:09:42.850 --> 00:09:47.130
For example, western countries have much stricter
policies when it comes to worker’s rights,

00:09:47.130 --> 00:09:48.550
minimum wage, and pollution.

00:09:48.550 --> 00:09:53.890
So, hopefully Foxconn’s business will become
cleaner as they diversify into western countries,

00:09:53.890 --> 00:09:55.830
but only time will tell.

00:09:55.830 --> 00:10:00.290
As for Terry, he’s currently 71 years old
and he still serves as the CEO and chairman

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:01.310
of Foxconn.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:06.779
He currently ranks in the top 500 richest
people in the world coming in at $6.15 billion.

00:10:06.779 --> 00:10:11.250
It’s a bit ironic that he’s only worth
$6 billion given that his company is the lifeblood

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:16.910
of many trillion dollar companies, but then
again, he only pays his employees $2 per hour,

00:10:16.910 --> 00:10:20.000
so maybe he doesn’t even deserve this in
the first place.

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:22.050
Do you guys think Terry deserves his wealth?

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:23.460
Comment that down below.

